A 3D COMSOL model is constructed to capture the acoustic and electronic behavior associated with the microphone. Figure 2b, c show the front view and perspective view of the 3D COMSOL model. The Solid Mechanics (solid) module is employed to capture the mechanical behavior of the diaphragm, the Electrostatics (es) module accounts for the piezoelectric and capacitive transduction, and the Thermoviscous Acoustics (ta) module describes the acoustic wave propagation and acoustic damping effects. These physics interfaces collectively capture the coupled multiphysics behavior of the proposed microphone. The diaphragm is mechanically clamped on the edge, and the bottom surface of the air gap is set as a rigid boundary with perforations. This rigid boundary is interfaced with \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
