				\begin{document}$${V}_{{bias}}$$\end{document}Vbias, while the top surface of the air gap is set as a Terminal port for capacitive transduction. The bottom interface of the AlN layer is set as a GND condition, while the bottom interface of the top electrode is set as a Terminal port for piezoelectric transduction. Figure 2d compares the frequency response of the hybrid device obtained from the LEM and COMSOL models in piezoelectric, capacitive, and hybrid transduction modes, respectively, with the reference value set at 1 V/Pa. The theoretical and simulated results match very well over a frequency of interest ranging from 100 Hz to 10 kHz. The diaphragm’s fundamental resonance lies above 30 kHz, which is beyond the plotted range, while the low-frequency cut-off caused by the venting hole and the charge-amplifier feedback network occurs below 20 Hz. Both the resonance and the low-frequency roll-off fall outside the measurement window and therefore do not appear in the figure.

(HF), and then the device is released. The 48% HF is highly aggressive and could attack all layers in the piezoelectric stack. To prevent the device from being damaged during the release step, a wax layer is applied to the front side of the substrate as a protective material. To mitigate the risk of diaphragm collapse and stiction, a critical point dryer (CPD) was employed to dry the device. The top view, bottom view, and SEM images of the fabricated hybrid microphone are shown in Fig. 5a−c. The basic parameters of the device are listed in Table 1. The film stress is obtained by measuring the substrate curvature before and after deposition using a Dektak profilometer, and the software automatically calculates the stress values based on Stoney’s equation. Table 2 lists the measured wafer-level stress values of deposited films.Fig. 5Images of the fabricated hybrid microphone.
