				\begin{document}$$1.86\,\mu V/\sqrt{{Hz}}$$\end{document}1.86μV/Hz, with a MDP of 31.8 dB. The SNR is calculated as 62.2 dB, which is 9.6 dB higher than the previously reported value of 52.6 dB27. However, the overall SNR in hybrid mode is not improved compared to the SNR in piezoelectric mode, because the overall noise floor in the hybrid mode is dominated by the contribution from the capacitive mode, which is more than five times that in the piezoelectric mode. The significantly higher noise floor observed in the capacitive mode—and by extension, the hybrid mode—is fundamentally governed by the interaction between the parasitic capacitances of the capacitive transduction branch and the interface amplifier. Specifically, the large parasitic elements, while inherently noiseless, substantially increase the total input capacitance seen by the amplifier. This interaction reshapes the noise contribution of the amplifier by altering the input impedance, thereby causing the amplifier’s input-referred noise to become the dominant source that determines the overall noise floor. Consequently, minimizing parasitic capacitance is identified as the promising strategy to mitigate this adverse circuit-noise coupling and significantly reduce the device noise level in future designs.

